INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('a1c04db5801794788e3c','Git amedex 2025.pdf','41','Gastroenterology','In a small town in Sydney, suddenly a number of people fell sick with bloody diarrhea, severe abdominal pain, and oliguria. In the Emergency
Department, most of them are severely dehydrated and confused. The panel of doctors recommended emergency blood tests. Which one of the
following, if present on test results, is diagnostic of the condition?','{"A":"A. Thrombocytopenia.","B":"OB. Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia.","C":"C. High creatinine levels.","D":"D. Severe ADAMTS13 deficiency.","E":"E. High LDH levels."}','B','Renal failure reflected by oliguria and abdominal pain following invasive diarrhea is the classic presentation of hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS). The
addition of CNS symptoms (confusion) to this constellation makes thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) another possibility if it is caused by
ischemia because of thrombi and not sodium derangement caused by severe dehydration. The exact etiology of HUS and TTP is unclear, but the role of
Shiga toxin in HUS and ADAMTS13 (a metalloproteinase) in TTP has been implicated.

HUS, and to some extent, TTP, commonly occur following a diarrheal illness with enterohemorrhagic Escherichia coli 0157:H7 and Shigella
dysenteriae serotype I. These bacteria, in addition to causing bloody diarrhea, are capable of secreting Shiga (Shigella) and Shiga-like toxins (E-
coli). These toxins can bind to specific cell membrane receptors, which, depending on the cell type, can result in:

1. Chemokine or cytokine secretion (colonic and renal epithelial cells)
2. Cellular activation (monocytes and platelets)
3. Secretion of unusually large Von Willebrand multimers (glomerular endothelial cells)

Clinical differentiation of hemolytic-uremic syndrome (HUS) and TTP can be problematic; however, central nervous system involvement is more common
in TTP, and more severe renal involvement in HUS.

In HUS, an antecedent history of diarrheal illness is often present. In fact, some authors suggest a clinical classification of HUS based on the presence or
absence of diarrhea.

In children, the distinction between HUS and TTP may be more important because general supportive measures (with dialysis as needed) are the
standard therapy for HUS, while TTP is treated with plasma exchange. However, In adults, HUS is also often treated with plasma exchange; therefore,
differentiating between HUS and TTP is not as important as in children.

There is not a single diagnostic test for HUS and TTP. These are diagnosed based on clinical presentation and presence of microangiopathic hemolytic
anemia presenting with:

1. Anemia, elevated bilirubin, and LDH (often significantly high)
2. Presence of schistocytes on peripheral smear

In this case, the presence of micro-angiopathic hemolysis is the most important finding that suggests either HUS or TTP. However, the distinction
between the two is neither possible with certainty nor necessary as the therapeutic approach is almost the same for both HUS and TTP in adults.

(Option A) Thrombocytopenia is almost always a feature of HUS and TTP, as it is in other conditions such as ITP; therefore, it is not diagnostic.

(Option C) Renal function tests, including creatinine, are part of the workup for suspected HUS or TTP but are not diagnostic because high creatinine
levels are seen in TTP and HUS as well as dehydration and many other conditions.

(Option D) ADAMTS13 is a metalloproteinase that cleaves Von Willebrand factor (VWF). Its deficiency results in circulating large multimers of VWF.
Large molecules of VWF multimers by adhering circulating platelets together lead to microthrombi in the organs, ischemia, and end-organ damage. The
majority of patients (>90%) with acquired TTP have circulating antibodies against ADAMTS13 making them ADAMTS13 deficient; however severe
ADAMTS13 deficiency is more common in sporadic forms rather than outbreaks. The test is time-consuming and more prognostic than diagnostic, so it is
not routinely ordered. Furthermore, ADAMTS13 deficiency alone does not seem to cause TTP, and a contributing factor such as pregnancy, infection,
drugs, etc. is required to trigger TTP.

(Option E) Regardless of the etiology, elevated LDH is seen in hemolysis. LDH is neither sensitive nor specific for HUS/TTP.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[41],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-36f6ca027ea9","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"a1c04db5801794788e3c","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":41,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"85e58dcead10b7405c79","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_page":2,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"09bcee114297dd903715","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_page":1,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"48ca170deacae945c19d","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Amedex 2023.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Amedex 2023.pdf","source_page":480,"source_qid":"50"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"a1c04db5801794788e3c","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":41,"supporting_pages":[41],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology","stem":"In a small town in Sydney, suddenly a number of people fell sick with bloody diarrhea, severe abdominal pain, and oliguria. In the Emergency\nDepartment, most of them are severely dehydrated and confused. The panel of doctors recommended emergency blood tests. Which one of the\nfollowing, if present on test results, is diagnostic of the con