been behaving peculiarly recently at work. Of importance, his father and paternal aunt died with the same symptoms at the ages of 50 and 53, respectively. Which one of the
following is most likely to result in a diagnosis?',NULL,'C','Neuropsychiatric problems in a young patient with a family history of such presentation suggestive of Wilson disease. Wilson disease is a rare (3 in 100,000) inherited
disease (autosomal recessive) caused by the accumulation of copper in the liver and central nervous system. The genetic basis is a mutation of a gene on chromosome 13
that codes for a copper-transporting ATPase, ATP7B. Over 200 mutations have been identified so far.

Total body copper content is 100-150mg, with an average 1-5mg/day intake. The dietary copper is absorbed in the proximal small bowel. Copper is incorporated into
ceruloplasmin in the liver. In Wilson disease, copper absorption and transport in the liver are intact, but incorporation into ceruloplasmin in the hepatocytes and biliary
excretion is impaired. This results in the accumulation of copper in the liver and later in other organs (e.g., CNS). The most common presentations are with liver disease or
neuropsychiatric disturbances. Asymptomatic patients are most often detected by family screening.

The clinical hallmark of Wilson''s disease is the corneal Kayser—Fleischer ring, which is present in 95% of patients with neurologic symptoms and over half of those without
neurologic symptoms.

Kayser-Fleischer ring - deposition of copper in the limbus

While most affected children present with hepatic involvement (hepatitis, cirrhosis, or fulminant liver failure), adults often present with neuropsychiatric manifestations as the
presenting symptoms.

Neuropsychiatric manifestations of Wilson''s disease include dysarthria, dysphagia, dyskinesias, purposeless stereotyped movements, dementia, Parkinsonism,
micrographia, and clumsiness. There can also be decreased memory, anger outbursts, labile emotions, decreased libido, and personality changes.

Other possible clinical features are:

* Coombs-negative hemolytic anemia
¢ Polyarthritis

¢ Hypermobile joints

¢ Grey skin

¢ Abortions

¢ Hypoparathyroidism

Diagnostic tests include serum copper (increased) and ceruloplasmin (decreased). It should be noted, however, that ceruloplasmin is an acute phase reactant and may be
increased in response to hepatic inflammation, pregnancy, estrogen use, or infection.)

The urinary copper excretion rate in patients with Wilson disease is greater than 100 mcg/d (normal<40mcq/day). Although the sensitivity and specificity of this test are
suboptimal for use as a screening test, it may be useful to confirm the diagnosis and to evaluate the response to chelation therapy.

NOTE—The criterion standard for diagnosing Wilson''s disease is liver biopsy. Copper levels of more than 250mcq per gram of dry weight of the liver in an asymptomatic
patient establish the diagnosis.

Chelation with D-penicillamine or trientine is the mainstay of therapy. Patients should follow diets with decreased sources of dietary copper.
CT scan of the head (option A) or, preferably, an MRI of the head (option B) are used to determine the degree of brain involvement in Wilson''s disease.
Lumbar puncture (option D) has no role in diagnosing Wilson''s disease.

Liver function tests (option E) are impaired in most patients with Wilson disease. However, these changes are nonspecific and can be seen in various other liver diseases;
hence, they are not diagnostic.',NULL,'Retain the source-era answer for question-bank study; verify current Australian guidance before clinical use.',NULL,NULL,'2025','v2.9.4',1,'text','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[74],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-dcdfec6d1e0a","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"7931a32b55535c0a8934","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":74,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"7c172755d6caae1b9398","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 76-100.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 76-100.pdf","source_page":27,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"c9de26472f61cd8f314b","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 76-100.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 76-100.pdf","source_page":28,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high') ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET source_id=excluded.source_id,source_question_no=excluded.source_question_no,source_page=excluded.source_page,domain=excluded.domain,system=excluded.system,topic=excluded.topic,clinical_task=excluded.clinical_task,stem_en=excluded.stem_en,answer_key=excluded.answer_key,source_explanation_en=excluded.source_explanation_en,memory_rule_en=excluded.memory_rule_en,image_ref=excluded.image_ref,source_year=excluded.source_year,import_version=excluded.import_version,active=1,options_mode=excluded.options_mode,provenance_json=excluded.provenance_json,current_answer=excluded.current_answer,current_answer_status=excluded.current_answer_status,current_evidence_json=excluded.current_evidence_json,currentness_risk=excluded.currentne