INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('c7bb22e150ce2f01da10','Git amedex 2025.pdf','76','Gastroenterology','A 45-year-old man presents with complaints of polyarthritis, impotence, and decreased libido. Which of the following investigations is the most appropriate step to
consider?','{"A":"OA. Iron studies.","B":"B. Fasting blood sugar.","C":"C. HFE gene testing.","D":"D. CT scan of the head.","E":"E. Prolactin level."}','A','It is crucial to consider hemochromatosis in patients with decreased libido and manifestations related to other body sites such as joints, liver, CNS, etc.
Hemochromatosis, through iron overload and deposition of iron in several organ systems, can present with the following features:

¢ Chronic hepatitis and cirrhosis (the most common cause of mortality).

¢ Abdominal pain.

¢ Arthralgia—often MCPs and large joints—is due to chondrocalcinosis and pseudogout. The pain is similar to that of osteoarthritis.
¢ Skin darkening (tanned skin) — iron deposition in the skin.

¢ Small testes, infertility, impotence, and decreased libido —small testes are due to hypopituitarism and/or liver disease. Iron deposition in gonads occurs but is not the
cause of hypogonadism.

¢ Damage to the pancreas and diabetes mellitus (known as bronze diabetes).

¢ Restrictive cardiomyopathy and ensued congestive heart failure (15% of patients). Other less common cardiac manifestations are supraventricular tachycardias,
atrial fibrillation, atrial flutter, and varying degrees of atrioventricular block.

¢ Accumulation of iron in the pituitary and panhypopituitarism.

¢ Hepatocellular carcinoma (hepatoma) — in 10% of cases with liver involvement.
¢ Osteoporosis (25% of cases) and osteopenia (41% of cases)

¢ Sparse body hair, especially pubic hair (62% of patients)

¢ Spoon nails (50% of cases)
NOTE -— the most common cause of death from hemochromatosis is liver cirrhosis, followed by cardiac disease, which affects approximately 15% of the patients.

lron studies are the most appropriate next step when hemochromatosis is suspected clinically.

The transferrin saturation (ratio of serum iron to iron binding capacity) reflects increased absorption of iron, which is the underlying biological defect in hereditary
hemochromatosis (HH). A fasting transferrin saturation greater than 45% is the most sensitive test for detecting early iron overload, but it is not diagnostic of HH. Ferritin
can be used to assess iron overload, but it is not as accurate because it is an acute phase reactant and may be elevated in response to several physiologic stresses,
alcohol consumption, and liver disease. Serum ferritin is abnormal when it is >250 pg/L in pre-menopausal women and >300 ug/L in men and post-menopausal women.

If fasting transferrin saturation or serum ferritin is increased on more than one occasion, HH should be suspected, even if there are no clinical symptoms or abnormal liver
function tests (LFT). In this situation, the HFE gene test (option C) should be considered the next and most diagnostic tool.

(Option B) Fasting blood sugar is a good step for this patient because diabetes is a possible feature of hemochromatosis; however, it is not diagnostic for the condition.
(Option D) CT scan of the head adds no diagnostic value in this patient.

(Option E) Although hemochromatosis can cause panhypopituitarism and decreased pituitary hormone levels, measurement of prolactin level alone does not add any
diagnostic value.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[76],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-30e5926d7597","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"c7bb22e150ce2f01da10","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":76,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"bd24793ae2fdd1ecb7a2","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 41-75.pdf","source_page":1,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"c7bb22e150ce2f01da10","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":76,"supporting_pages":[76],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology","stem":"A 45-year-old man presents with complaints of polyarthritis, impotence, and decreased libido. Which of the following investigations is the most appropriate step to\nconsider?","options":{"A":"OA. Iron studies.","B":"B. Fasting blood sugar.","C":"C. HFE gene testing.","D":"D. CT scan of the head.","E":"E. Prolactin level."},"source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"It is crucial to consider hemochromatosis in patients with decreased libido and manifestations related to other body sites such as joints, liver, CNS, etc.\nHemochromatosis, through iron overload and deposition of iron in several organ systems, can present with the following features:\n\n¢ Chronic hepatitis and cirrhosis (the most common cause of mortality).\n\n¢ Abdominal pain.\n\n¢ Arthralgia—often MCPs and large joints—is due to chondrocalcinosis and pseudogout. The pain is sim