INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('661190c68649591f3d26','Git amedex 2025.pdf','94','Gastroenterology','A72-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a complaint of perianal pain for the past two days. His anal area is illustrated in the accompanying
photograph. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?','{"A":"OA. Thrombosed internal hemorrhoid.","B":"B. Thrombosed external hemorrhoid.","C":"C. Rectal carcinoma.","D":"D. Crohn''s disease of the anus.","E":"E. Perianal abscess."}','A','Traditionally, hemorrhoids are classified as internal and external; however, some authors believe that since these two have different origins and mechanisms of
development, they are better-termed hemorrhoids (instead of internal hemorrhoids) and perianal hematomas (instead of external hemorrhoids). Perianal hematoma and
external hemorrhoids are often interchangeably used.

Hemorrhoid (internal hemorrhoid):

The anus is mainly lined by discontinuous masses of spongy vascular tissue termed anal cushions, which contribute to anal closure and differentiating flatus from stool.
Viewed from the lithotomy position, these cushions are located at 3, 7, and 11 o’clock. These cushions are attached together and to the surrounding structures by
supporting fibromuscular tissue. Hemorrhoids occur when these structures become bulky and protruded due to gravity, straining, or increased tone of the anal sphincter
(unlike the common belief, the hemorrhoid is not a varicose anal vein).

Bulky and protruded cushions are at risk of trauma from hard stool and bright red bleeding from the capillaries of the underlying lamina propria.

Constipation and straining are the most common causes of hemorrhoids; however, bowel habits are normal in many patients. Congestion from a pelvic tumor, pregnancy,
congestive heart failure, nephrosis, or portal hypertension plays a role in only a minority of patients.

Hemorrhoids are classified as follows:

¢ 18t degree — remains in the rectum.
e and degree — prolapses through the anus on defecation but reduces spontaneously afterward.
¢ 3''4 degree — like 2"4 degree but needs digital reduction.

e qth degree — remains prolapsed persistently.

Hemorrhoids are painless unless acute thrombosis superimposes, in which case, they become painful. Thrombosed hemorrhoids are managed conservatively with
analgesics, stool softeners, bed rest (elevation of the bed foot), and ice packs for 2-3 weeks. The drawback of this method is the long time off work. Hemorrhoidectomy is
the second option if conservative management is not an option for any reason.

Perianal hematoma (external hemorrhoid)

A perianal hematoma is not a hemorrhoid; however, it is usually called an external hemorrhoid. It presents as a painful, tense blue swelling at the anal verge caused by a
recent thrombosis of an anal vein, often after straining at stool.

The picture in the question shows a fleshy red lesion protruding out of the anus, consistent with an (internal) hemorrhoid. The presence of pain suggests acute thrombosis.
(Option B) External hemorrhoid has a different appearance.

(Option C) Although hemorrhoids can be associated with rectal carcinoma, the lesion itself is not a carcinoma. Moreover, a carcinoma this large would have been
associated with more pronounced systemic and local symptoms.

(Option D) It is important to note that for every anorectal lesion, a thorough investigation should be conducted in an attempt to exclude serious underlying pathologies
such as inflammatory bowel disease or malignancies.

(Option E) Perianal abscesses present with painful and tender red perianal swelling and induration, not a lesion protruding out of the anus.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[94],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-316bc4797f47","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"661190c68649591f3d26","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":94,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"661190c68649591f3d26","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":94,"supporting_pages":[94],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology","stem":"A72-year-old man presents to the emergency department with a complaint of perianal pain for the past two days. His anal area is illustrated in the accompanying\nphotograph. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":{"A":"OA. Thrombosed internal hemorrhoid.","B":"B. Thrombosed external hemorrhoid.","C":"C. Rectal carcinoma.","D":"D. Crohn''s disease of the anus.","E":"E. Perianal abscess."},"source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"Traditionally, hemorrhoids are classified as internal and external; however, some authors believe that since these two have different origins and mechanisms of\ndevelopment, they are better-termed hemorrhoids (instead of internal hemorrhoids) and perianal hematomas (instead of external hemorrhoids). Perianal hematoma and\nexternal hemorrhoids are often interchangeably used.\n\nHemorrhoid (internal hemorrhoid):\n\nThe anus is mainly lined by discontinuous masses of spongy v