INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('f2c07b21db79396133a6','Git amedex 2025.pdf','108','Gastroenterology','Which of the following is the most common way hepatitis B infection is transmitted in Australia?','{"A":"OA. Sexual intercourse.","B":"B. Transfusion of blood and blood products.","C":"C. Injecting drug use.","D":"D. Vertical transmission from the mother to the infant.","E":"E. Breast feeding."}','A','Hepatitis B virus is a blood-borne virus, meaning that the virus should enter the bloodstream to cause infection. Modes of transmission include:

¢ sharing contaminated objects that pierce the skin or mucous membranes, such as needles, tattoo equipment, body-piercing equipment,
acupuncture equipment, razor blades, and toothbrushes

¢ sexual contact (heterosexual or homosexual, more common with homosexual contact)

¢ perinatal transmission from an infected mother to her infant (vertical transmission)

¢ needlestick injuries

¢ contact between infective fluid and mucous membranes, such as a splash of blood into eyes or mouth

¢ invasive medical or dental procedures if there has been inadequate infection control

¢ transfusion of infected blood or blood products

¢ human bites and other direct contact with the blood or open spores of an infected person

Of these routes, some are more common than others in different parts of the world, depending on the regional prevalence of the chronic hepatitis B
infection:

¢ Low-prevalence areas: Such areas include the United States, Canada, Western Europe, Australia, and New Zealand. The prevalence of hepatitis B
in such areas is $2% (0.1-2%). Less than 12% of HBV infected individuals live in these areas. The most common routes of transmission in these
countries are sexual transmission and percutaneous transmission.

¢ Intermediate-prevalence areas: Sexual and percutaneous transmission and transmission during delivery are the major transmission routes in areas
of intermediate prevalence (rate of 3-5%). These regions include Eastern and some areas of Northern Europe, Japan, some Mediterranean
countries, Latin and South America, and Central Asia.

¢ High-prevalence areas: Such areas include China, Southeast Asia, sub-Saharan Africa, the Pacific Islands, Parts of the Middle East, and the
Amazon Basin. The prevalence is 28% (often 10-20%). Perinatal infection, including vertical transmission from the mother to the infant, is the
predominant transmission mode.

Australia has a chronic hepatitis B prevalence of less than 0.5% and is among the low-prevalence regions of the world. The most common mode of
transmission of hepatitis B infection in Australia is heterosexual and homosexual sexual intercourse, with the latter being associated with a higher risk of
transmission. This is followed by percutaneous transmissions, such as in injecting drug use, and non-sterile procedures, such as piercing, tattooing, etc.

(Option B) In Australia, screening of blood and organ donors using serological and, subsequently, nucleic acid amplification testing has virtually
eliminated the risk of transmission of hepatitis B through blood transfusion and organ transplants.

(Option C) Injecting drug use predominates other modes of transmission in newly infected individuals with hepatitis C in Australia. It is also an important
mode of transmission of hepatitis B after sexual intercourse.

(Option D) Perinatal transmission, including vertical transmission from the mother to the infant, is the predominant route of transmission of hepatitis B
infection in areas with a high prevalence of HBV infection. In such areas, the prevalence is 28% (generally 10-20%). These areas include China,
Southeast Asia, Indonesia, sub-Saharan Africa, the Pacific Islands, parts of the Middle East, and the Amazon Basin. Due to perinatal care and
administration of the hepatitis B vaccine within 12 hours of birth, this route of transmission is not common in Australia.

(Option E) Breastfeeding does not appear to be a significant transmission route, nor do fecal-oral and vector-borne modes.
NOTE - Saliva may contain levels of virus that are likely to be infective only if inoculated directly into tissue (ocular or mucous membranes). The risk

of transmission by inadvertent inoculation by other means, such as toothbrush, razor, etc., or through close personal contact in households where one
or more infected persons reside is low but not negligible.','{"source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","supporting_pages":[108],"source_qid":null,"duplicate_group_id":"exact-68c933749916","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"f2c07b21db79396133a6","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_page":108,"source_qid":null},{"record_id":"93527108c394b2bacf84","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX 2025.09更新包/AMEDEX 2025 GIT 1-40.pdf","source_page":15,"source_qid":null}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v27_same_page_five_structural","text_method":"tesseract_retry_200dpi_psm3","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"f2c07b21db79396133a6","source_file":"Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_relative_path":"AMEDEX2025+redbook+handbook图片题/Git amedex 2025.pdf","source_year":2025,"source_page":108,"supporting_pages":[108],"source_qid":null,"domain_hint":"Gastroenterology","stem":"Which of the following is the most common way hepatitis B infection is transmitted in Australia?","options":{"A":"OA. Sexual intercourse.","B":"B. Transfusion of blood and blood products.","C":"C. Injecting drug use.","D":"D. Vertical transmission from the mother to the infant.","E":"E. Breast feeding."},"source_answer":"A","source_explanation":"Hepatitis B virus is a blood-borne virus, meaning that the virus should enter the bloodstream to cause infection. Modes of transmission include:\n\n¢ sharing contaminated objects that pierce the skin or mucous membranes, such as needles, tattoo equipment, body-piercing equipment,\nacupuncture equipment, razor blades, and toothbrushes\n\n¢ sexual contact (heterosexual or homosexual, more common with homosexual contact)\n\n¢ perinatal transmission from an infe